.TH PROVOST 1 "2026-10-20" "provost 2.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
provost \- turn command output into data
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B provost run
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fB--\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs...\fR]
.br
.I command
|
.B provost
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B provost
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIFILE\fR
.br
.B provost
\fIsubcommand\fR [\fIargs...\fR]
.br
.B provost
\fIsubcommand\fR \fB--help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B provost
captures a command's output, auto-detects its format (via a library of 200+
extract strategies), and presents it as a tidy, one-row-per-record dataset in a
git-backed store. Every capture is a commit; every dataset row carries a
globally-unique index so it can be traced back to its source.
.SH CAPTURE OPTIONS
Accepted by
.B run
and by injection (piped stdin or a \fIFILE\fR):
.TP
.BI --name " NAME"
File into dataset \fINAME\fR instead of the default name (see
.BR "DATASET NAMES" ).
.TP
.BI --min-confidence " N"
Commit threshold 0\-101 (default: \fBPROVOST_MIN_CONFIDENCE\fR, 50). A capture
the engine is less sure of goes to the \fBunsorted\fR triage bucket.
.TP
.BI --min-coverage " N"
Quality gate (default: \fBPROVOST_MIN_COVERAGE\fR, 80; 0 turns it off): a
generic parse that keeps under \fIN\fR% of the input's lines goes to the
\fBunsorted\fR triage bucket. See \fBQUALITY GATE\fR.
.TP
.BI --min-consistency " N"
Quality gate (default: \fBPROVOST_MIN_CONSISTENCY\fR, 70; 0 turns it off): a
generic parse whose columns are under \fIN\fR% type-consistent goes to the
\fBunsorted\fR triage bucket.
.TP
.BI --plugin-dir " DIR"
Add an extra strategy directory to detection (or \fBPROVOST_PLUGIN_DIR\fR), so
a wrapping project's strategies compete without being symlinked into
\fI~/.config/provost/strategies\fR.
.TP
.B --multi
File every dataset the input holds (or \fBPROVOST_MULTI=1\fR).
.TP
.BR --auto-multi ", " --no-auto-multi
Before the quality gate holds a parse, try segmenting the input and file the
parts when that is clearly better (the default, or
\fBPROVOST_AUTO_MULTI=1\fR); \fB--no-auto-multi\fR holds it straight away.
See \fBAUTO-MULTI\fR.
.TP
.BR --sync ", " --wait
Capture in the foreground and print the resulting dataset and \fI#N\fR range.
This is the default when stderr is not a terminal (scripts, pipelines).
.TP
.B --async
Capture in a detached background process; the prompt returns at once. This is
the default when stderr is a terminal.
.TP
.BR -q ", " --silent
No progress output (or \fBPROVOST_QUIET=1\fR).
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B run [\fIoptions\fR] [--] \fIcommand\fR [args...]
Execute a command, capture its combined output as a commit, auto-detect the
format, and file it as a dataset named after the command. The output is capped
at \fBPROVOST_MAX_INPUT\fR bytes, like injection. provost exits with the
command's own exit status.
.TP
.I command
| \fBprovost\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
Inject piped stdin (or a \fIFILE\fR argument) the same way as
.BR run .
A leading run of \fB#%\fR lines on injected input is a metadata prologue:
it is peeled off before detection (it cannot corrupt a parse), stored with the
capture as \fIcontext.txt\fR, and shown verbatim by
.BR "provost source" .
Run with no arguments from a terminal (nothing piped in),
.B provost
prints its help instead of waiting on stdin.
.TP
.B source \fI#N\fR
Show the original captured input behind dataset row \fI#N\fR.
.TP
.B control \fR[\fIdir\fR]
Make \fIdir\fR (default: cwd) its own capture instance; captures run inside it
route there, and every capture's commit snapshots the directory's contents as
context (\fIcontext/manifest.tsv\fR).
.TP
.B uncontrol \fR[\fIdir\fR]
Release a controlled directory (its data is kept).
.TP
.B instances
List controlled directories.
.TP
.B export \fR[\fIfile\fR]
Bundle the whole store (datasets + capture commits) into a portable file.
.TP
.B import \fIfile\fR \fR[\fIdest\fR]
Reconstitute a bundle elsewhere to browse.
.TP
.B log \fR[\fB--failed\fR]
List captures (the git history). \fB--failed\fR lists background captures that
failed (and marks them seen).
.TP
.B restore \fIid\fR|latest
Bring a capture's commit back up (detached); \fBlatest\fR returns to the tip.
While a capture is restored, new captures and \fBrm\fR are refused.
.TP
.B ls
List datasets.
.TP
.B show \fINAME\fR
Present a dataset as a tidy table (leading \fB#N\fR is the row index).
For the \fBunsorted\fR bucket the base64 \fBraw\fR column is decoded to text.
.TP
.B rm [\fB-f\fR|\fB--yes\fR] [\fB--no-purge\fR] \fINAME\fR...
Delete one or more datasets. Removes each named dataset file, prunes its rows
from the provenance map, and commits the deletion (the git history and
\fBrestore\fR still reach the pre-deletion state). By default it also purges
each capture that fed only removed dataset(s); a \fB--multi\fR capture that
still feeds a surviving dataset is kept and its \fImeta.tsv\fR updated.
\fB--no-purge\fR/\fB--keep-captures\fR keeps every capture. Prompts unless
\fB-f\fR/\fB--yes\fR. Aliases: \fBdelete\fR, \fBdrop\fR.
.TP
.B stats \fINAME\fR [analyze-args]
Statistics on a dataset (\fB--all\fR for every analysis).
.TP
.B triage
Show the low-confidence / unmatched bucket, raw input decoded to text
(\fBprovost source #N\fR for the exact original bytes).
.TP
.B doctor
Check the extract/analyze engines and the store; lists failed background
captures.
.TP
.B help
Print help to stdout. \fIsubcommand\fR \fB--help\fR prints that command's usage.
.SH DATASET NAMES
.B provost run
\fIcmd\fR ... files into a dataset named after \fIcmd\fR's basename, looking
through the wrappers \fBsudo\fR, \fBdoas\fR, \fBenv\fR (with its
\fIVAR\fR=\fIval\fR arguments), \fBnice\fR, \fBionice\fR, \fBnohup\fR,
\fBtime\fR, \fBcommand\fR, \fBexec\fR, \fBstdbuf\fR and \fBtimeout\fR (with its
duration) and their options; \fBxargs\fR is not a wrapper. So
.B provost run sudo df -h
files into \fBdf\fR. Injected input is named after the winning parser (e.g.
\fBcsv\fR, \fBfree\fR, \fBaligned-columns\fR). \fB--name\fR overrides both.
Names consist of letters, digits, \fB.\fR, \fB_\fR and \fB-\fR, start with a
letter or digit, and never contain \fB..\fR. Unmatched input goes to
\fBunsorted\fR. With \fB--multi\fR each dataset is named after its strategy,
prefixed \fINAME\fR\fB.\fR for \fBrun\fR or \fB--name\fR; so are the parts of a
capture segmented by \fBAUTO-MULTI\fR, except that with a \fINAME\fR the part
whose columns match the single parse's keeps \fINAME\fR.
.PP
A capture whose columns differ from the existing dataset's is reconciled into
it (columns unioned) when the overlap is at least
\fBPROVOST_RECONCILE_OVERLAP\fR percent, else it goes to a variant
\fINAME\fR\fB.v2\fR, \fB.v3\fR, ...
.SH LINE COVERAGE
A capture reports its \fIcoverage\fR: the share of the input's non-blank lines
that reached the dataset (shown after the confidence and recorded as
\fBcoverage\fR in the capture's \fImeta.tsv\fR; not measured above 128 KB).
Confidence says how sure the engine is of the format; coverage says how much of
the input the parse kept. When the parse ranked first keeps under 60% of the
lines \(em one section of a multi-section report \(em the engine tries a
lossless key/value parse of the whole report (one row per key, list item and
section) and files that instead if it keeps materially more. A capture still
filed under 60% says so; the whole input is kept with the capture either way
(\fBprovost source\fR).
.SH QUALITY GATE
A capture whose \fIgeneric\fR parse keeps under \fB--min-coverage\fR percent of
the input's lines (default 80), or whose columns are under
\fB--min-consistency\fR percent type-consistent (default 70), is held back in
the \fBunsorted\fR triage bucket with the reason, instead of filed as a table:
a likely-wrong table is worse than none. On the rated benchmark sets this held
18 of 48 wrong parses and no fully right one. Tool-specific parsers and strict
grammars (json, yaml, xml, toml) are exempt, and a measurement that is absent
(very large inputs) never holds a capture back. Set either to 0 to turn that
check off. Before a capture is held, \fBAUTO-MULTI\fR tries segmenting it.
.SH AUTO-MULTI
When the quality gate would hold a parse for low coverage or low consistency,
provost first asks the engine to segment the input, as \fB--multi\fR does, and
files the parts instead when the split is clearly better: it is a real split
(two or more datasets); every part passes the gate on its own (confidence,
coverage, consistency); together the parts keep at least \fB--min-coverage\fR
percent of the lines and 10 points more than the single parse; no part is a
table under positional A/B/C... columns only; a part read by the parser the
gate rejected has judged column types; and parts of three or more lines hold at
least half the lines, so the input is not shattered into fragments. Otherwise
the capture is held as before and the reason segmenting did not help is printed
and recorded as \fBauto_multi\fR (\fIdeclined: ...\fR) in \fImeta.tsv\fR.
.PP
A segmented capture says so in its ok line and records \fBauto_multi\fR (why
the single parse was held), \fBsingle_strategy\fR, \fBsingle_coverage\fR and
\fBsingle_consistency\fR, the parts' joint \fBcoverage\fR, and each part's
\fBdataset_coverage\fR and \fBdataset_consistency\fR. The parts are named as
with \fB--multi\fR (\fINAME\fR\fB.\fR\fIstrategy\fR, or the strategy for a
nameless injection), except that with a \fINAME\fR the part whose columns
match the single parse's (\fBPROVOST_RECONCILE_OVERLAP\fR) keeps dataset
\fINAME\fR, so repeated captures of one command keep their rows in one dataset
whether or not a given capture segments. Only captures the gate would hold pay
for the second engine run. \fB--no-auto-multi\fR or \fBPROVOST_AUTO_MULTI=0\fR
turns it off.
.SH COLUMN-TYPE CONSISTENCY
A parse can keep every line and still put values under the wrong headers: a
date split over three columns, a multi-word name spread over Name / Id /
Version, a key: value file read as a two-column table. The engine types every
cell (number, size, date/time, address, version, path, word, ...) and records
the share of cells that match their column's type as \fBconsistency\fR in the
capture's \fImeta.tsv\fR (not measured above 128 KB; a cell holding a word cut
in half counts as a mismatch). When the generic parse ranked first is below
95% and another generic parse within 20 confidence points keeps as many lines,
types its columns better (95% or more) and mixes fewer columns, the capture is
filed with that parse instead.
.SH FOREGROUND AND BACKGROUND
From an interactive terminal a capture runs in the background; from a script or
pipeline it runs in the foreground, so
.B "cmd | provost; provost show x"
never races. A failed background capture is appended to the store's
\fI.async-failures.log\fR (its input and full log are kept in the spool
directory it names); every later provost command prints a one-line warning
until the failure is seen with \fBprovost log --failed\fR or \fBprovost doctor\fR.
.SH STORE
By default captures go to a global git store at
.BR $PROVOST_HOME ", or " ~/.provost
if unset, so you can capture from any directory. Point
.B PROVOST_HOME
at a shared, group-writable directory to collaborate; a lock serialises
concurrent writers, and a store owned by another user is trusted by provost's
own git calls. A controlled directory (see
.BR control )
overrides the global store while you are inside it.
.PP
The store holds only data. The extract/analyze engines and the transform
module are loaded only from provost's own installation directory, never from
the store (which a shared store's other members can write to). provost's git
calls also disable hooks and fsmonitor.
.PP
Capture ids and row indices are never reused (not after \fBrm\fR, not after
\fBrestore\fR). Runtime files (the lock, the failure log, temporary files) are
listed in the store's \fI.gitignore\fR and never committed; stores created by
older versions are migrated on the next write.
.PP
The store lock records host, pid, uid and time. It is broken as stale only when
it was taken on this host and its process no longer exists, or when it is older
than \fBPROVOST_LOCK_MAX_AGE\fR seconds.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Numeric settings must be whole numbers; any other value is refused with exit
status 2.
.TP
.B PROVOST_HOME
Global store directory (default: ~/.provost).
.TP
.B PROVOST_WORKSPACE
Explicit store override (wins over PROVOST_HOME).
.TP
.B PROVOST_MIN_CONFIDENCE
Commit threshold 0\-100 (default: 50); 101 holds every capture back for triage.
.TP
.B PROVOST_MIN_COVERAGE
Quality gate: minimum line coverage, in percent, for a generic parse to be
filed (default: 80; 0 turns it off).
.TP
.B PROVOST_MIN_CONSISTENCY
Quality gate: minimum column-type consistency, in percent, for a generic parse
to be filed (default: 70; 0 turns it off).
.TP
.B PROVOST_PLUGIN_DIR
Extra strategy directory for detection (same as \fB--plugin-dir\fR on
inject/run).
.TP
.B PROVOST_ASYNC
\fB1\fR always captures in the background, \fB0\fR always in the foreground;
unset or \fBauto\fR (the default) backgrounds only when stderr is a terminal.
.TP
.B PROVOST_MULTI
File every dataset an input holds (default 0; same as \fB--multi\fR).
.TP
.B PROVOST_AUTO_MULTI
\fB1\fR (the default): before the quality gate holds a parse, try segmenting
the input (see \fBAUTO-MULTI\fR); \fB0\fR: hold it straight away (same as
\fB--no-auto-multi\fR).
.TP
.B PROVOST_MAX_INPUT
Maximum input bytes, for injection and \fBrun\fR output (default: 10485760).
.TP
.B PROVOST_LOCK_TIMEOUT
Seconds to wait for the store lock (default: 30).
.TP
.B PROVOST_LOCK_MAX_AGE
Seconds after which a store lock is presumed stale even when its owner cannot be
checked, e.g. one taken on another host (default: 3600).
.TP
.B PROVOST_RECONCILE_OVERLAP
Percent column overlap needed to reconcile a changed schema into the same
dataset instead of a \fB.v\fIN\fR variant (default: 60; 0\-101).
.TP
.B PROVOST_QUIET
\fB1\fR suppresses progress and ok messages (same as \fB--silent\fR).
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where temporary and background spool directories are created (default: /tmp).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on a runtime error (including a git failure \- provost never
reports a capture git did not record); 2 on a usage error (unknown command or
option, invalid dataset name or setting); for \fBrun\fR, the command's own exit
status once it has been captured; 127 when the command to run is not found.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
free -m | provost                 # capture + auto-detect + file (dataset 'free')
provost run df -h                 # capture a command as a commit (dataset 'df')
provost ls                        # list datasets
provost show df                   # present as a tidy table
provost source 5                  # original input behind row #5
provost stats df                  # statistics on the dataset
provost run --name disks df -h    # file into a dataset of your choosing
provost log --failed              # background captures that failed
provost export ~/store.bundle     # portable export
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The provost design is documented in
